 conversation with them over a couple of  minutes  that was such a small thing i do that  all the time because it feels natural  to spend this very you know precious  time with these students  trying to encourage them and give them a  positive influence but  to know that that can have a very deep  and lasting effect on someone  that's pretty special and i always want  my students to take that lesson away  from their time at school  wow eddie thank you we have  about six minutes left to go and  jane asks not this one um  do you think that kindness solves most  problems encountered in the classroom  and playground and possibly the world or  is that stretching it too much  um i i'm an optimist so i can definitely  come alongside the idea that kindness  especially  undeserved kindness like when i was  talking about forgiveness before  it is powerful and it can have really  deep  and amazing effects not just on an  individual but indeed on a whole class a  school  a society i do want to recognize though  that sometimes the problems that we face  around us  to reckon with their reality um  a simple kind act sometimes isn't enough  and i don't want us to  you know imagine that some of the  systemic problems around us  can simply be solved by one act here or  another i think for example  and that the fact that i often work with  regional schools across new south wales  and in rural and remote settings  sometimes the challenges that  communities face out there  are far deeper and run further into a  community's  past its history they're far more  profound than can simply be undone  by a few individual acts here and there  we have to reckon  with the fabric of society the history  that's brought us here  um and the things that have been done  that we need to reckon with i mean it  was recently nadoc week and i think  about  um when the national when the apology to  the stolen generations was given  that was a powerful marker and  it was a start of a conversation but  that in 